with the collapsed roof and upper wall debris, shows pressure-dissolution features, stylolites in mudbrick grains, and a transition to a more massive, consolidated fabric—signs that the deep burial load finally arrived, by which time the room was already a solid, competent block. So the direct, empirical answer to your question is: we know because the evidence is locked in the soil itself, in the form of pristine dust laminae, intact floor assemblages, and a complete lack of pressure-fracturing in the lower fill. The physics works because the actual history, readable in the grains, matches the gradual-fill scenario and contradicts the crushed-with-partial-fill hypothesis. Ancient rooms are time capsules because they were embalmed in dust before they could be broken.
