ore directly relevant: In the same Pompeii ash layers, archaeologists found entire bakeries with carbonized loaves of bread still in the ovens, stamped with the baker's mark. Amphorae (tall, thin-walled terracotta jars) stand upright on floors, still sealed with their cork stoppers and contents inside. Wooden furniture, papyrus rolls, even eggs have been found intact. If the pumice and ash had simply dropped a crushing weight onto these fragile objects, they would be shattered. The reason you see them in museums all over the world is precisely because the granular fill crept up around them gradually, supporting them from all sides before any significant weight accumulated. This isn't a theory archaeologists had to retrospectively invent; it's a visual fact you can observe in any photo of the Pompeii excavations. The objects are in place, unbroken, with fine ash laminations draped over them. ### The terracotta warriors of Xi'an: standing in a wooden trench You've probably seen pictures of the Terracotta Army in China. Over 8,000 life-sized ceramic soldiers, each unique, were buried in pits with wooden roof structures. Over time, the wooden beams rotted and the earth collapsed into the pits, covering the figures. The soldiers are intact, with their original paint traces, weapons in hand, standing in ranks. If the collapsing roof and subsequent burial had simply crushed them, they wouldn't be standing. They would be a pile of ceramic shards. The key is that the collapsing earth initially filled the spaces around them, packing them in before the full weight of the overlying soil settled. The soil supported their legs and torsos equally from all sides, preventing buckling. The proof is in the upright figures themselves. You can visit them, see them with your own eyes, and understand the physics without a guide explaining pressure equalization. ### The "Frozen in Time" houses of Akrotiri On the Greek island of Santorini, the Minoan settlement of Akrotiri was buried by a v